<div class="Section1">

<h1>Sepsis SERS Biosensor — Experiment Roadmap &amp; Action Plan</h1>
<p class="subtitle"><strong>Sukesh P (M24IM1007)</strong> — M.Tech Joint IDRP, IIT Jodhpur &amp; AIIMS Jodhpur</p>
<p class="subtitle">PI: Prof. Ajay Agarwal (IIT Jodhpur) &middot; Co-PI: Prof. (Dr.) Neeraj (AIIMS Jodhpur) &middot; Co-PIs: TCS</p>
<p class="subtitle">Prepared: 11 September 2026, from your own project reports and planning documents.</p>

<p>Everything below is pulled directly from your existing reports (<code>Consolidated_Experimental_Report_AllSessions</code>, <code>Updated_Project_Progress_Report_With_Experiments</code>, <code>Document_A_Experimental_Execution_Plan_SOP</code>, <code>Document_B_Scientific_Rationale_Decision_Log</code>, <code>Experiment_Plan_LPS</code>, <code>Experiment_Plan_LTA</code>, <code>Next_Experiment_Plan_BareAg_NAM_Optimization</code>, <code>LPS_Substrate_Strategy</code>). No quantities, catalog numbers, or results below are invented — anything genuinely open is marked <strong>STILL UNRESOLVED / CONFIRM</strong>.</p>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 0 — Honest Status Check: What "Not Started" Actually Means</h2>
<p>You said you haven't started any experiments yet. That's true in the sense that matters most: <strong>no biomarker (NAM, LTA, or LPS) has yet been successfully detected by SERS.</strong> But three real lab sessions already happened (12, 13, 14 August 2026), plus a full computational/reference-spectroscopy stage before that. You're not starting from zero — you're starting from a well-characterized negative result with a clear, literature-grounded next move. Here's exactly where things stand.</p>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 1 — What Has Actually Been Completed</h2>

<h3>Stage 1 — Pure-standard DFT + powder Raman (complete, off-substrate)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Biomarker</th><th>DFT method</th><th>Powder Raman result</th></tr>
<tr><td>NAM</td><td>B3LYP/6-31+G(d,p), full molecule, Opt+Freq+Raman</td><td>25 s integration, 80% power, SNR = 95.91. <strong>51 peaks matched to DFT</strong>, RMSE 2.12 cm&#8315;&sup1;, mean error 0.38 cm&#8315;&sup1; (&plusmn;10 cm&#8315;&sup1; tolerance). Strong result.</td></tr>
<tr><td>LPS</td><td>B3LYP/6-31G(d), 5 fragments (Kdo, Heptose, D-glucosamine, myristic acid, phosphoric acid), UltraFine/Tight grid</td><td>60 s, 20% power, 30 accumulations. NNLS composite fit: R&sup2; = 0.180, RMSE 0.185, Pearson 0.508. Fragment contributions: Kdo 47.3%, Heptose 25.8%, D-glucosamine 15.3%, myristic acid 11.7%, phosphoric acid 0.0%.</td></tr>
<tr><td>LTA</td><td>B3LYP/6-31G(d), 3 fragments, SMD water solvation</td><td>Acquisition parameters <strong>not available in the current report</strong>. NNLS fit: <strong>R&sup2; = &minus;0.2943 (poor)</strong>, Pearson 0.3448, RMSE 0.1928 — qualitative region-level agreement only (phosphate, carbohydrate, CH-deformation, amide bands); the 3-fragment model does not capture full polymeric LTA.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Reminder (your earlier instruction, still true):</strong> the old "51 NAM peaks" and "LPS R&sup2;=0.18" figures are powder-Raman-vs-DFT results, not SERS results. Don't let them stand in for SERS detection when you talk about this project.</p>

<h3>Stage 2 — SERS substrate work (new, three real sessions)</h3>
<p><strong>Fabrication used throughout:</strong> Si cleaned acetone &rarr; IPA &rarr; DI (5 min sonication each); native oxide stripped in ~10% HF dip (4.1 mL 49% HF + 15.9 mL DI), 30&ndash;60 s; one-step MACE bath = 0.170 g AgNO&#8323; + 8.6 mL conc. HF, made up to 50 mL DI (&asymp; <strong>20.0 mM AgNO&#8323;</strong>, confirmed by calculation; HF molarity &asymp;4.8 M <strong>if</strong> stock is 49% — <strong>STILL UNRESOLVED</strong>, HF stock concentration was never recorded). Etch times tested: 2, 5, 10 min, room temperature.</p>
<p><strong>Instrument:</strong> B&amp;W Tek/Metrohm i-Raman Plus, BWS465-785H spectrometer + BTC665N-785H-SYS probe, actual laser wavelength 784.92 nm. Power reported only as arbitrary "power level" 5 or 10 — <strong>mW at sample is STILL UNRESOLVED</strong> across every session and every planning document. This is the single most repeated open item in your own records — resolve it before your next session if at all possible (check the BWSpec software's live power readout, or the printed spec on the laser head).</p>

<h4>Session 1 (12 Aug) — Fabrication + R6G + NAM, 177 spectra</h4>
<ul>
<li>R6G: all 8 standard bands (612, 774, 1183, 1310, 1362, 1509, 1575, 1650 cm&#8315;&sup1;) detected on all 3 etch-time substrates, high SNR. <strong>2-min etch gave the strongest signal</strong> (1509 cm&#8315;&sup1;: 21,118 counts / SNR 450 — ~48&times; the 5-min substrate). 10-min beat 5-min but not 2-min — <strong>etch-time effect is not monotonic</strong>, and only one substrate was made per condition, so batch-to-batch reproducibility was never tested.</li>
<li>NAM: measured on all 3 substrates &times; 2 power levels, target bands 930 cm&#8315;&sup1; (ring breathing) and 871 cm&#8315;&sup1; (CH&#8322; wagging) primary. Best individual hits: SNR 3.0 (1023 cm&#8315;&sup1;, isolated) and SNR 3.3 (930 cm&#8315;&sup1;, isolated) — <strong>neither reproduced across spots or conditions and both were correctly rejected</strong>. <strong>Verdict: NAM NOT DETECTED.</strong></li>
</ul>

<h4>Session 2 (13 Aug) — Instrument/Si reference characterization, 45 spectra</h4>
<ul>
<li>Si TO phonon found at <strong>517.8 cm&#8315;&sup1;</strong> vs. textbook 520&ndash;521 cm&#8315;&sup1; &rarr; fixed <strong>&minus;2 to &minus;3 cm&#8315;&sup1; calibration offset</strong> &rarr; <strong>&plusmn;5 cm&#8315;&sup1; tolerance</strong> now applied project-wide.</li>
<li>Peak FWHM at best condition &asymp; 7.2 cm&#8315;&sup1; &asymp; 4&ndash;5 detector pixels &rarr; basis for the <strong>"reject any peak narrower than 3 pixels" artifact rule</strong> now mandatory before any peak call.</li>
<li>SNR scales <em>faster</em> than &radic;t (5.9&times; actual vs. 2.2&times; shot-noise-predicted going 5&rarr;25 s) &rarr; <strong>longer integration is an unusually effective, underused lever</strong> on this specific instrument.</li>
<li><strong>NAM molecular-weight correction discovered here:</strong> earlier work used 275.25 g/mol; the correct value is <strong>293.27 g/mol</strong> (PubChem CID 5462244, matches Sigma A3007, CAS 10597-89-4). 1 mg/mL NAM = <strong>3.41 mM</strong>, not 3.63 mM as previously logged — old NAM molarity figures are ~6.5% too high. <strong>Use 293.27 g/mol going forward.</strong></li>
</ul>

<h4>Session 3 (14 Aug) — LTA detection attempt, 15 spots</h4>
<ul>
<li>Apparent 1095 cm&#8315;&sup1; signal (SNR 7&ndash;9, 3 spots) initially looked positive.</li>
<li>Pixel-level re-examination: the feature occupied <strong>exactly one detector pixel (index 640)</strong>, identical position across all 3 candidate spectra — a fixed-pattern detector artifact, not a molecular signal. <strong>Corrected verdict: LTA NOT DETECTED.</strong> This finding is what produced the mandatory despiking/pixel-width rule now used everywhere.</li>
</ul>

<h4>LPS — not yet attempted on any substrate. This is genuinely your first blank slate.</h4>

<h3>Root-cause analysis for the NAM/LTA null results (ranked, from your own reports)</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Most probable:</strong> NAM has no thiol/strong Ag-binding group (amide-blocked nitrogen) and no resonance at 785 nm — weak adsorption + weak intrinsic Raman cross-section, both at once. This is a structural deficiency, not a fixable instrument problem.</li>
<li>Droplet spreading on the strongly hydrophilic black-Si surface (coffee-ring effect carrying analyte outside the measured spot) — a real contributing factor, being corrected via hydrophobic barrier rings + smaller confined drop volumes (2 &micro;L).</li>
<li>Bulk concentration too low — judged unlikely as primary cause (1 mg/mL / 3.41 mM is already high by SERS standards), but not fully excluded.</li>
<li>Substrate not SERS-active — <strong>excluded</strong> (R6G confirms strong activity, SNR to 450).</li>
<li>Instrument miscalibration — <strong>excluded</strong> (Session 2 Si reference data confirms correct operation).</li>
</ol>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 2 — Immediate Priority Roadmap: Your Next Three Experiments, In Order</h2>
<p>Your own decision log (<code>Document_B</code>) sets this priority order explicitly: <strong>LPS = Priority 1</strong> (best published precedent, most transferable protocol), <strong>LTA = Priority 2</strong>, <strong>NAM optimization = ongoing track, gated on MPBA availability for a real fix</strong>. Do these in order — a working detection on <em>any</em> one biomarker de-risks the whole platform and gives you a validated substrate to apply to the others.</p>

<h3>EXPERIMENT 1 (DO THIS FIRST): LPS Label-Free SERS Detection</h3>
<p><strong>Why LPS first:</strong> it is the <em>only</em> one of your three analytes with a fully transferable, published, label-free SERS protocol on a silver substrate — exact volumes, drying method, acquisition settings, and a peak table, adapted from Wu, Zhao &amp; Zughaier (2021), <em>Biosensors</em> 11(7):234, DOI 10.3390/bios11070234. Lipid A's six saturated fatty-acyl chains are both strong Raman scatterers <em>and</em> drive real physisorption onto Ag — the exact combination NAM lacks.</p>

<div class="caution"><strong>Reagent — CONFIRMED:</strong> Sigma-Aldrich <strong>L2630</strong>, <em>E. coli</em> O111:B4 LPS, phenol-extracted, CAS 93572-42-0, 10 mg pack (&#8377;12,773.50 indicative). <strong>CAUTION — acutely toxic:</strong> GHS06, H300 "fatal if swallowed," Acute Toxicity Category 2 (Oral). Weigh only in a fume hood/powder enclosure; never on an open bench. Locked 2&ndash;8&deg;C storage; collect all contaminated tips/tubes/substrates as toxic waste, never to drain.</div>

<p><strong>Substrate — recommended new architecture, "Sensor A" (two-step MACE):</strong> your current one-step AgNO&#8323;/HF process couples Si etching and Ag growth into a single uncontrolled event. Both strongest published LPS benchmarks (Wu 2021; Yang 2022, <em>Nanoscale</em> 14(24):8806) used silver-nanorod arrays where Ag morphology is engineered <em>independently</em> of the support — not replicable without e-beam evaporation, but the underlying principle (decoupling Si structure from Ag growth) is replicable with chemistry you already have.</p>

<p><strong>Day 1 — Fabrication (~3 hours, 4 Si pieces from one wafer):</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th><th>Quantity/time</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Clean: acetone &rarr; IPA &rarr; DI</td><td>5 min sonication each</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Oxide strip</td><td>4.1 mL 49% HF + 15.9 mL DI (20 mL); 30&ndash;60 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>MACE etch, all 4 pieces together</td><td>0.170 g AgNO&#8323; + 8.6 mL HF, to 50 mL DI; <strong>10 minutes</strong> (longer than your usual 2 min — deeper nanostructure)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Rinse, N&#8322; dry</td><td>&ge;30 s running DI</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Set aside Piece 1</strong> — do not strip it</td><td>This is your one-step (M0) comparison control</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>HNO&#8323; Ag strip — Pieces 2, 3, 4</td><td>Dilute HNO&#8323; until the dark Ag deposit visibly lifts; rinse thoroughly</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Ag re-deposition, separate baths/times</td><td>5 mM AgNO&#8323; + 1 mL 49% HF in 50 mL. Piece 2: 30 s &middot; Piece 3: 60 s &middot; Piece 4: 120 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Rinse, N&#8322; dry, label immediately</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Day 2 — Validation + detection (~4 hours):</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Same-session blank, all 4 pieces</td><td>3 spots each, 30 s, 3 acc., power 10, 400&ndash;1800 cm&#8315;&sup1;</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>R6G 0.01 mM, all 4 pieces</td><td>3 spots each. <strong>GO/NO-GO GATE.</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>Compare 1509 cm&#8315;&sup1; across pieces 1&ndash;4</td><td>Pick the winner. If all re-deposited pieces lose badly to Piece 1, the HNO&#8323; strip is damaging the structure — fall back to "Sensor B" below.</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>Hydrophobic barrier rings on winning piece</td><td>3&ndash;5 rings, ~3&ndash;4 mm apart, dry ~5 min</td></tr>
<tr><td>13</td><td>LPS 100 &micro;g/mL, 2.0 &micro;L/spot</td><td>9 spots — primary detection attempt</td></tr>
<tr><td>14</td><td>LPS negative control on <strong>unetched bare Si</strong></td><td>3 spots — mandatory (proves any signal is SERS, not ordinary Raman)</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>If detected, dilution series</td><td>10 / 1 / 0.1 &micro;g/mL, 5 spots each</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>Analyze</td><td>Despike &rarr; baseline-correct &rarr; per-spot criteria &rarr; only then average</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>LPS solution prep</strong> (1 mg into 1.00 mL pyrogen-free water = 1 mg/mL stock): vortex 2 min &rarr; sonicate 15 min &rarr; vortex 30 s before every draw. Haziness is normal — do not filter. Serial dilutions (100 &micro;L + 900 &micro;L water each step) give 100/10/1/0.1 &micro;g/mL working levels; re-vortex + re-sonicate at every dilution step, not just at stock stage.</p>

<p><strong>Target peaks</strong> (Wu 2021, Table 1 — measured SERS on Ag, not calculated):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>cm&#8315;&sup1;</th><th>Assignment</th><th>Priority</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>989</strong></td><td>&beta;(CH), carbohydrate</td><td><strong>PRIMARY</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>1330</strong></td><td>&delta;(CH), phospholipid</td><td><strong>PRIMARY</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>550, 680, 850, 1131, 1450</td><td>ring/fatty-acid/saccharide/lipid modes</td><td>secondary</td></tr>
<tr><td>543, 552, 981</td><td>enteric-LPS variants (E. coli/Salmonella/Vibrio)</td><td>expect these closer to O111:B4 than the N. meningitidis values above</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Detection verdict:</strong> DETECTED requires <em>both</em> 989 and 1330 cm&#8315;&sup1;, SNR&ge;3, &ge;3 pixels wide, in &ge;5 of 9 spots, within &plusmn;5 cm&#8315;&sup1;. Do not accept a single band alone.</p>

<p><strong>Performance context:</strong> Yang 2022 reports LPS detectable at <strong>8.75 pg per measurement</strong> (&asymp;4.4 ng/mL in a 2 &micro;L drop). Your planned 100 &micro;g/mL delivers ~200 ng in 2 &micro;L — roughly <strong>23,000&times; more material</strong> than their detection threshold. This is exactly why LPS is the right first target: enormous margin for error.</p>

<p><strong>Materials needed beyond what you have:</strong> HNO&#8323; (65&ndash;70% conc., for the Ag strip — confirm you have this), calcium gluconate gel (confirm in-date), pyrogen-free water (confirm availability; ordinary DI may carry background endotoxin).</p>

<div class="caution"><strong>Fallback if Sensor A fails the R6G gate:</strong> "Sensor B" — HF/H&#8322;O&#8322; oxidant-controlled MACE. Brief AgNO&#8323;/HF seeding dip (5 mM AgNO&#8323; + 1 mL HF in 50 mL, 60 s) to deposit catalyst particles, then etch in ~4.8 M HF + 0.1 M H&#8322;O&#8322; for 5/15/30 min (a directly-reported working condition). New reagent needed: 30% H&#8322;O&#8322;. <strong>CAUTION:</strong> never pre-mix H&#8322;O&#8322; and HF for storage — prepare immediately before use.</div>

<h3>EXPERIMENT 2: NAM Bare-Ag Optimization — AgNO&#8323; Concentration Series</h3>
<p><strong>Rationale:</strong> R6G works strongly on your existing substrate, so the problem is not substrate SERS-activity — it's that NAM has no chemical reason to sit inside a hotspot. This experiment tests whether nanostructure geometry (particle size/density/gap spacing, which literature says tracks with AgNO&#8323; concentration) can partially compensate, while explicitly <em>not</em> touching NAM concentration or adding capture chemistry in the same round — that would make results uninterpretable.</p>

<p><strong>Four groups, all at fixed 2-min etch, fixed NAM concentration (use corrected 293.27 g/mol MW):</strong></p>
<table>
<tr><th>Group</th><th>AgNO&#8323; target</th><th>Mass in 50 mL</th><th>Expected effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>A — Low</td><td>5 mM</td><td>0.0425 g</td><td>Larger, sparser particles, wider gaps</td></tr>
<tr><td>B — Baseline repeat</td><td>20 mM</td><td>0.170 g</td><td>Reproduces 12 Aug result; reproducibility check</td></tr>
<tr><td>C — Medium-high</td><td>40 mM</td><td>0.340 g</td><td>Smaller, denser particles — best candidate to show improvement if hotspot density is the limiter</td></tr>
<tr><td>D — High</td><td>80 mM</td><td>0.680 g</td><td>Risk of dendritic clustering (literature caution)</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Everything else held constant: 2-min etch, 8.6 mL HF/50 mL bath, same NAM stock/drop volume (2&ndash;3 &micro;L), 5 s integration, 3 accumulations, one fixed power level, &ge;3 spots per condition, same-session blank every time (fixes the gap where your 2-min substrate had <em>no</em> matched blank on 12 Aug).</p>

<p><strong>Decision rule:</strong> Step 1 — discard any group whose R6G check doesn't clear SNR&ge;10 at 1509 cm&#8315;&sup1; (fabrication problem, not data). Step 2 — among survivors, prefer lowest spot-to-spot R6G variability. Step 3 — rank by how many NAM primary bands (930, 871 cm&#8315;&sup1;) clear SNR&ge;3 in &ge;2 of 3 spots. Step 4 — magnitude only as a tiebreaker.</p>

<p><strong>If NAM is still not detected after Groups A&ndash;D</strong> (a realistic, informative outcome): run the conditional NAM concentration series (5&times;/10&times; = 18.2/36.3 mM using the corrected MW) on the single best AgNO&#8323; condition — <em>only after</em> Groups A&ndash;D are analyzed, not in parallel. No signal even at 10&times; points squarely at capture chemistry (MPBA) as the real fix, not further nanostructure tuning.</p>

<p><strong>Explicitly not recommended for this round:</strong> persulfate (PDS) in the bath — no literature precedent for one-step Ag/HF MACE, real risk of re-oxidizing deposited Ag&#8304;.</p>

<h3>EXPERIMENT 3: LTA Bare-Ag Detection (repeat, with corrected artifact protocol)</h3>
<p><strong>Reagent — CONFIRMED:</strong> Sigma-Aldrich <strong>L2515</strong>, LTA from <em>S. aureus</em>, CAS 56411-57-5, 5/10/25 mg packs (&#8377;20,664.93 for 5 mg). No GHS pictogram, but <strong>WGK 3 (severely water-hazardous)</strong> — do not pour LTA solutions or rinse water to drain.</p>

<div class="caution"><strong>CAUTION — aqueous stability is the tightest constraint in this protocol:</strong> stable only <strong>2 days at 4&deg;C</strong>, or 2 months as single-use frozen aliquots at &minus;20&deg;C. Prepare fresh on measurement day, or freeze single-use 100 &micro;L aliquots immediately. This may partly explain the 14 Aug null result if that solution had aged — <strong>confirm your 14 Aug stock's age before reusing it; if in doubt, discard and re-prep.</strong></div>

<p><strong>Mandatory pre-analysis step:</strong> despike every spectrum (reject any peak &lt;3 pixels wide) before calling any peak — this is what the pixel-640 correction demands.</p>

<p><strong>Prep:</strong> 1.0 mg LTA + 1.00 mL DI &rarr; vortex 2 min &rarr; sonicate 15 min &rarr; aliquot into 10&times; 100 &micro;L single-use portions, freeze unused ones. Dilution series: 500/100/10/1 &micro;g/mL (start higher than the LPS series — LTA has weaker published precedent, so the first goal is simply establishing whether <em>any</em> signal is obtainable).</p>

<p><strong>Target bands</strong> (evidence-tiered — no complete published LTA SERS spectrum exists anywhere):</p>
<table>
<tr><th>cm&#8315;&sup1;</th><th>Assignment</th><th>Evidence tier</th></tr>
<tr><td>1080&ndash;1095</td><td>&nu;<sub>s</sub>(PO&#8322;&#8315;), phosphate</td><td>Literature-confirmed for the functional group (not LTA-specific — also nucleic acids, LPS, wall teichoic acid)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1225&ndash;1250</td><td>&nu;<sub>as</sub>(PO&#8322;&#8315;)</td><td>Literature-confirmed for the functional group</td></tr>
<tr><td>1450</td><td>CH&#8323;/CH&#8322; lipid deformation</td><td>Confirmed via LPS Table 1 (shared lipid-tail chemistry)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1131</td><td>&nu;(C-C) fatty acid</td><td>Confirmed via LPS Table 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>850</td><td>&nu;(C-O-C) saccharide</td><td>Confirmed via LPS Table 1</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Detection rule — stricter than a single band:</strong> because 1080&ndash;1095 cm&#8315;&sup1; is <em>not</em> LTA-specific, a valid detection needs <strong>both</strong> a phosphate band (1080&ndash;1095 or 1225&ndash;1250) <strong>and</strong> a lipid band (1450 or 1131), SNR&ge;3, &ge;3 pixels wide, in &ge;3 of 9 spots. A lone phosphate band is "possibly detected," never "detected."</p>

<p><strong>Matrix:</strong> blank (3 spots) &rarr; R6G gate (3) &rarr; LTA 500/100 &micro;g/mL (9 spots each) &rarr; LTA 10/1 &micro;g/mL (5 spots each) &rarr; blank + 500 &micro;g/mL repeat on a 2nd substrate (reproducibility check). ~42 spectra, budget 3.5&ndash;4 hours.</p>

<p><strong>If bare Ag gives nothing but R6G is strong:</strong> run a cysteamine-SAM arm (2-aminoethanethiol, Sigma M6500) — a thiol-anchored, positively-charged &minus;NH&#8323;&#8314; surface is a more rational capture strategy for LTA specifically than for NAM, since LTA's phosphodiester backbone is genuinely negatively charged. <strong>Do not add this in the same round as the bare-Ag baseline</strong> — test bare Ag first.</p>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 3 — Conditional / Follow-On Experiments (once Part 2 produces a result)</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>NAM 5&times;/10&times; concentration series</strong> — only after the AgNO&#8323; series is fully analyzed (see Experiment 2).</li>
<li><strong>Cysteamine SAM for LTA</strong> — only if bare-Ag LTA fails (see Experiment 3).</li>
<li><strong>Cross-analyte comparison</strong> — once at least one biomarker shows real signal, run LPS + LTA + NAM together on substrates from a single fabrication batch, matched concentration, identical settings, to determine which biomarker the platform is actually best suited to.</li>
<li><strong>LOD/LOQ determination</strong> — only after a first positive detection, from a real concentration series (your own reports flag that you should not compute a statistical LOD from fewer than 5 concentration points with replicates — report an "observed lowest-detected concentration" instead until you have that).</li>
<li><strong>Independent substrate replicates</strong> at the winning condition — never yet tested; only one substrate was fabricated per condition across all three sessions so far, so batch-to-batch fabrication reproducibility is still an open question.</li>
<li><strong>Fabrication-to-measurement interval logging</strong> — track how long each substrate sits between etching and measurement (freshly-etched Ag tarnishes); this was never systematically tracked before.</li>
</ul>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 4 — Previously-Discussed Ideas Not Yet Implemented (the "dig up the ideas" ask)</h2>
<p>These are documented in your own planning files as real options that were deliberately deferred, not forgotten:</p>

<h4>Capture chemistry (the actual fix for NAM):</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>MPBA (4-mercaptophenylboronic acid)</strong> — binds NAM's diol/carboxylic-acid functionality via the boronic-acid group, anchoring it in SERS-active regions. Flagged everywhere as <em>the</em> long-term fix for NAM once available. <strong>Explicit methodological caution from your own decision log:</strong> record the MPBA-only spectrum first to rule out spectral interference, since the functionalization itself could occupy SERS-active sites — this must be evaluated by controlled comparison, not assumed to help.</li>
<li><strong>Cysteamine SAM</strong> — interim, more rational for LTA than NAM (see Experiment 3).</li>
</ul>

<h4>Alternative Si/Ag fabrication architectures catalogued but not yet built (from <code>LPS_Substrate_Strategy</code>, ranked #2&ndash;#5 after Sensor A):</h4>
<ul>
<li>Pre-made SiNWs + galvanic-displacement Ag coating — strongest reported numbers found (EF 2.3&times;10&#8312;, R6G LOD 10&#8315;&#185;&#8308; M), but full-text parameters are paywalled/unverified.</li>
<li>Porous Si + Ag immersion plating — has a concrete, directly-reported recipe to copy verbatim (10 mM AgNO&#8323;, 5 min); NIR-relevant SERS demonstrated.</li>
<li>HF/H&#8322;O&#8322; oxidant-controlled MACE (Sensor B, already detailed above as your fallback).</li>
<li>MACE + photonic (UV/laser) reduction — highest verified EF (~1&times;10&#8312;), reusable substrate, but needs a controlled photoreduction setup you haven't used before.</li>
<li>Lower-priority/less-supported options also catalogued: two-step MACE w/H&#8322;O&#8322; plating-then-etch, AgNP pre-deposition before MACE, KOH/IPA hierarchical texturing, thermal sintering to remove Ag remanence.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Downstream project directions (not started, per your own progress report's "remaining work" list):</h4>
<ul>
<li>Expand the pure-standard Raman reference library to additional bacterial components beyond NAM/LTA/LPS.</li>
<li>Validate against whole cultured bacterial isolates, not just purified compounds.</li>
<li>Machine-learning classification of SERS spectra — your literature review already found precedent (RamanNet reaching 100% differentiation accuracy on 11 endotoxin types in Yang 2022; a 2025 follow-up "DeepRaman," Heliyon, PMC11870271, not yet analyzed in detail).</li>
<li>Evaluation on real clinical patient samples.</li>
<li>Portable point-of-care prototype development.</li>
</ul>

<p>These map directly onto your project's original 5 objectives — objectives 3 (AI/ML), 4 (clinical validation), and 5 (point-of-care prototype) are <strong>all explicitly "not yet started"</strong> in your own progress report, and objective 2 (SERS sensing) is "in progress" pending the experiments in Part 2.</p>

<hr class="rule">

<h2>PART 5 — Open Items You Should Resolve (safety- and data-quality-critical, consolidated from every document)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Item</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Exact laser power in mW</strong> for power levels "5" and "10"</td><td>Every acquisition-settings table in every plan depends on this; needed to compare against published mW figures (Wu 2021 used 100 mW) and decide integration time. Check BWSpec's live display or the BTC665N-785H-SYS spec label.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><strong>HF stock concentration</strong> (assumed 49% throughout — never confirmed)</td><td>Affects exact HF molarity in every bath; check the reagent bottle label.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Pyrogen-free water availability</strong></td><td>Needed for LPS work specifically — ordinary DI may carry background endotoxin and contaminate your blank.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Vacuum desiccator availability</strong></td><td>Preferred drying method for LPS; air-dry is an acceptable logged substitute if unavailable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Whether your 14 Aug LTA stock is still usable</strong></td><td>Sigma's stability data (2 days at 4&deg;C) suggests it may have degraded — confirm prep date before reusing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td><strong>HNO&#8323; and H&#8322;O&#8322; availability</strong> (new reagents, needed for Sensors A and B respectively)</td><td>Confirm you have these before Day 1 of the LPS experiment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Si wafer specs</strong> (resistivity, doping, orientation, thickness)</td><td>Never recorded anywhere in your files; orientation is conventionally assumed (100) but unconfirmed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Calcium gluconate gel</strong> — confirm in-date and on-site</td><td>Mandatory first aid for every HF step across all three experiments.</td></tr>
</table>

